</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In the missing data imputation techniques realm, several methodological approaches have been proposed. The simplest strategies rely on basic statistics such as mean, median, or mode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. While computationally efficient, they fail to capture the underlying complex distribution of the data, leading to biased imputations and loss of variability. More advanced statistical approaches, such as Multivariate Imputation by Chained Equations (MICE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, iteratively update missing values through conditional models, thereby reducing bias. However, MICE is computationally expensive in high-dimensional settings and sensitive to model misspecification. Thus, ML–based methods have gained prominence by using feature interactions for imputation. Widely used examples include MissForest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> and k-Nearest Neighbors (kNN) imputer, valued for their accuracy and simplicity. More recently, HyperImputer was proposed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, which dynamically selects and combines models for improved imputations. Nonetheless, ML-based approaches often struggle with scalability and high proportions of missingness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. Recently, DL methods have emerged to capture higher-order dependencies. Representative models include Generative Adversarial Imputation Nets (GAIN) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, which adapts Generative Adversarial Networks to generate realistic imputations; ReMasker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, which reconstructs masked entries using contextual neural representations; and DiffPuter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, which applies diffusion models for iterative refinement. While these methodologies are robust, they require large sample sizes, demand significant computational resources, and pose a risk of overfitting when applied to smaller datasets, a frequent issue in healthcare contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To summarize and highlight the strengths of our proposal, Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Interpretable Generative and Discriminative Learning for Multimodal and Incomplete Clinical Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides a comparative overview of the key characteristics of the reviewed approaches with respect to the three main challenges addressed in this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Proposed model</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the limitations of the state-of-the-art, we introduce <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">O</span>ptimized <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>parse <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>nference and <span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>econstruction for <span id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>nterdependent <span id="S3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>paces (OSIRIS). Our proposal uses a combination of generative and discriminative formulations, enabling learning in multimodal scenarios, with support for semi-supervised training<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          The semi-supervised setting refers to the training process where the model have access to all the data (both train and test) except the test labels.</span></span></span> and robust handling of missing data through imputation. These two last aspects are closely related, as the test labels are masked and treated as missing values, allowing the model to work with the entire dataset without accessing the test labels. In addition, OSIRIS identifies two latent representations (or cross-modality factors): one captures discriminative components that facilitate the identification of predictive biomarkers, and the other captures generative components that enables efficient data imputation and enhances our understanding of relationships across different modalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider a multimodal classification problem with <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> data samples observed across <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> different modalities or views, denoted by <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{(\mathbf{x}_{n,:}^{\{\mathcal{M}\}},\mathbf{t}_{n,:})\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\mathbf{x}_{n,:}^{\{\mathcal{M}\}},\mathbf{t}_{n,:})\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\{1,2,\dots,M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\{1,2,\dots,M\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{n,:}^{(m)}\in\mathbb{R}^{D^{(m)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{n,:}^{(m)}\in\mathbb{R}^{D^{(m)}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th observation from the <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th modality, and <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{n,:}\in\{0,1\}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{n,:}\in\{0,1\}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is its one-hot encoded label over <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> possible classes. Throughout the paper, we use <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\times D</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix. Moreover, we will denote as <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> the diagonal matrix filled by vector <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=[\eta_{1},\dots,\eta_{N}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=[\eta_{1},\dots,\eta_{N}]</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\eta}=\text{diag}(\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\eta}=\text{diag}(\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>; and as <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Tr}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Tr}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> the trace operator. Finally, for variational developments, the mean and covariance of a random variable (r.v.) <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> will be denoted as <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\eta\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\eta\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Generative Model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to learn a latent representation that models both inter-view and intra-view structure while capturing the discriminative information for the classification task.
To this end, we introduce OSIRIS, a novel ML model combining generative and discriminative utilities. OSIRIS uses two interdependent latent spaces: a generative latent space, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\G\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\G\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which captures shared and unique structures across modalities, enabling missing data imputation and semi-supervised learning; and a task-oriented latent space, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Z\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Z\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times K}</annotation></semantics></math>, which captures discriminative features optimized for classification. The graphical model in Figure <a href="#S3.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Generative Model ‣ 3 Proposed model ‣ Interpretable Generative and Discriminative Learning for Multimodal and Incomplete Clinical Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> illustrates how the different input views contribute to the construction of both latent spaces, which in turn jointly model the output view. <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Besides, for further explanations, in order to be consitent with the multimodal FA literature, the term loadings were used for variables that map the latent space to the observed data while weights were used for variables that map from observed data to the latent space.</span> We now describe in detail the role and structure of each latent space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09513v1/OSIRIS_m_view.svg" id="S3.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/353;" width="476" height="353"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Input view/modality</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09513v1/Graphic_Model_new-5.svg" id="S3.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/486;" width="476" height="486"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Output view/modality</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Diagram of the graphical model of OSIRIS and a zoom into each input view. Grey circles denote observed variables, white circles unobserved variables. Blue font denotes generative variables and green font task-oriented variables. Rectangles represent node groups dependent on the view’s feature space.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The generative latent space</span> is modeled with a standard Gaussian prior, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{S})</annotation></semantics></math>, and is used to reconstruct each input view as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\rm n,:}^{(m)}\mid\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}\mathbf{V}^{(m)\top},\psim^{-1}\mathbf{I}_{D^{(m)}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>ψ</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mmultiscripts><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\rm n,:}^{(m)}\mid\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}\mathbf{V}^{(m)\top},\psim^{-1}\mathbf{I}_{D^{(m)}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{(m)}\in\mathbb{R}^{D^{(m)}\times S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{(m)}\in\mathbb{R}^{D^{(m)}\times S}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">loadings</span> for modality <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psim</annotation></semantics></math> is a view-specific noise precision parameter drawn from a Gamma prior: <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psim\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha^{\psim},\beta^{\psim}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psim\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha^{\psim},\beta^{\psim}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. This formulation enables the generation of new data samples and imputation of missing observations, while naturally supporting semi-supervised learning using the shared latent structure across all input views.
To jointly enforce sparsity over both the latent factors and the input features, we place a double Automatic Relevance Determination (ARD) prior on the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">generative loadings</span>, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{v}_{\rm d,s}^{(m)}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\left(\lambda_{d}^{(m)}\delta^{(m)}_{s}\right)^{-1}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>v</mtext><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{v}_{\rm d,s}^{(m)}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\left(\lambda_{d}^{(m)}\delta^{(m)}_{s}\right)^{-1}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{s}^{(m)}\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha_{s}^{\delta^{(m)}},\beta_{s}^{\delta^{(m)}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{s}^{(m)}\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha_{s}^{\delta^{(m)}},\beta_{s}^{\delta^{(m)}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{d}^{(m)}\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha_{d}^{\lambda^{(m)}},\beta_{d}^{\lambda^{(m)}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{d}^{(m)}\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha_{d}^{\lambda^{(m)}},\beta_{d}^{\lambda^{(m)}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> control sparsity across latent dimensions and input features, respectively, following the ARD principle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the task-oriented latent space</span> is directly inferred from the observed data as a sparse, linear combination of all views:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\sum_{m=1}^{M}\mathbf{x}_{\rm n,:}^{(m)}\mathbf{W}^{(m)\top},\tau^{-1}\mathbf{I}_{K}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\sum_{m=1}^{M}\mathbf{x}_{\rm n,:}^{(m)}\mathbf{W}^{(m)\top},\tau^{-1}\mathbf{I}_{K}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{(m)}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times D^{(m)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{(m)}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times D^{(m)}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the view-specific <span id="S3.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">weights</span>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a shared noise precision parameter drawn from a Gamma prior: <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha^{\tau},\beta^{\tau}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha^{\tau},\beta^{\tau}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
To impose sparsity across features and latent dimensions, we define a double ARD prior:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k,d}^{(m)}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,\left(\phi_{k}^{(m)}\gamma_{d}^{(m)}\right)^{-1}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k,d}^{(m)}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,\left(\phi_{k}^{(m)}\gamma_{d}^{(m)}\right)^{-1}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}^{(m)}\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha_{k}^{\phi^{(m)}},\beta_{k}^{\phi^{(m)}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}^{(m)}\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha_{k}^{\phi^{(m)}},\beta_{k}^{\phi^{(m)}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{d}^{(m)}\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha_{d}^{\gamma^{(m)}},\beta_{d}^{\gamma^{(m)}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{d}^{(m)}\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha_{d}^{\gamma^{(m)}},\beta_{d}^{\gamma^{(m)}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> force sparsity over each latent component and feature, respectively. Note that, to enhance model interpretability and reduce overfitting, ARD priors are placed over the <span id="S3.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">loadings</span> <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S3.SS1.p3.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">weights</span> <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math>, enforcing shrinkage on irrelevant latent dimensions and features. Thus, this hierarchical prior structure enables ARD for both reconstruction (via the generative space) and classification (via the task-oriented space).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">To model the output view</span>, OSIRIS computes the continuous representation <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math> of the output view label <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math> as a linear combination of the discriminative and generative latent variables (see Figure <a href="#S3.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Generative Model ‣ 3 Proposed model ‣ Interpretable Generative and Discriminative Learning for Multimodal and Incomplete Clinical Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}=\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}\mathbf{U}^{\top}+\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}\mathbf{V}^{\mathbf{Y}\top}+\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{y}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mi>𝐔</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐠</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mi>𝐕</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}=\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}\mathbf{U}^{\top}+\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}\mathbf{V}^{\mathbf{Y}\top}+\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{y}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times K}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">represents the discriminative loadings</span>, whose prior is defined as <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{\rm:,k}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{\rm:,k}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{C})</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\mathbf{Y}}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\mathbf{Y}}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times S}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the <span id="S3.SS1.p4.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">generative loadings</span> of the output view and, therefore, follows Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.1 Generative Model ‣ 3 Proposed model ‣ Interpretable Generative and Discriminative Learning for Multimodal and Incomplete Clinical Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>); and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{y}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\textbf{0},\eta^{-1}\Ic\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">0</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{y}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\textbf{0},\eta^{-1}\Ic\right)</annotation></semantics></math> models the output variability, with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha^{\eta},\beta^{\eta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>η</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>η</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\sim\Gamma\left(\alpha^{\eta},\beta^{\eta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To complete the generative process, the observed categorical labels <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math> are linked to their corresponding continuous representations <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math> through a Bayesian logistic regression as</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\mathbf{t}_{\rm n,:}|\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:})=e^{\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}\mathbf{t}_{\rm n,:}}\sigma(-\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mi>𝐭</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{t}_{\rm n,:}|\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:})=e^{\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}\mathbf{t}_{\rm n,:}}\sigma(-\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)=(1+e^{-x})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)=(1+e^{-x})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Including both latent spaces in the generation of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math> serves two main purposes. First, treating the output as a generative view allows the latent variable <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math> to capture label-related structure, enabling semi-supervised learning and robust inference even when label data is partially missing, since <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math> is informed by the observed views. Second, this dual formulation encourages a clean separation of roles: the discriminative latent space <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimized to retain only task-relevant information, while <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math> absorbs complementary or noisy variability. This setup acts similarly to a PCA-like reconstruction term, denoising the label view and improving the discriminative focus of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math>, ultimately enhancing performance, as confirmed in our experimental results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><math id="S3.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> update rules for key variables obtained using the mean-field approximation.
</span></figcaption>
<div id="S3.T2.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:135.1pt;vertical-align:-65.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-46.7pt,18.3pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<p id="S3.T2.5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:438.4pt;height:171.6pt;vertical-align:-83.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">


<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_thead">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:5pt 1.0pt;"><span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variable</span></span>
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:5pt 1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">distribution</span></span>
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:5pt 1.0pt;"><span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameters</span></span></span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:5pt 1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:5pt 1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}|\langle\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}\rangle,\Sigma_{\mathbf{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}|\langle\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}\rangle,\Sigma_{\mathbf{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:5pt 1.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:5pt 1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\mathbf{Z}}^{-1}=\langle\tau\rangle\Ik+\langle\eta\rangle\langle\mathbf{U}^{\top}\mathbf{U}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\mathbf{Z}}^{-1}=\langle\tau\rangle\Ik+\langle\eta\rangle\langle\mathbf{U}^{\top}\mathbf{U}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:5pt 1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\langle\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}\rangle=\left(\langle\tau\rangle\sum_{m}^{M}\mathbf{x}_{\rm n,:}^{(m)}\langle\mathbf{W}^{(m)}\rangle^{\top}+\langle\eta\rangle\left(\langle\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}\rangle-\langle\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}\rangle\langle\mathbf{V}^{\mathbf{Y}}\rangle^{\top}\right)\langle\mathbf{U}\rangle\right)\Sigma_{\mathbf{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝐔</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝐙</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{z}_{\rm n,:}\rangle=\left(\langle\tau\rangle\sum_{m}^{M}\mathbf{x}_{\rm n,:}^{(m)}\langle\mathbf{W}^{(m)}\rangle^{\top}+\langle\eta\rangle\left(\langle\mathbf{y}_{\rm n,:}\rangle-\langle\mathbf{g}_{\rm n,:}\rangle\langle\mathbf{V}^{\mathbf{Y}}\rangle^{\top}\right)\langle\mathbf{U}\rangle\right)\Sigma_{\mathbf{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span></span>
