</span></span></span></p>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin the study by analyzing OSIRIS’ performance in different configurations to evaluate the individual contributions of the discriminative and generative spaces to prediction and assess the benefits of semi-supervised learning. Thus, after training OSIRIS in both semi-supervised and fully supervised modes, we compared its performance using three prediction setups: (i) both <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math> (default setup), (ii) only <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (iii) only <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. As reported in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3 Application to multimodal clinical data ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Interpretable Generative and Discriminative Learning for Multimodal and Incomplete Clinical Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, two main conclusions emerge: first, semi-supervised learning yields significantly better results than fully supervised training, highlighting the benefit of including unlabeled data through <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math>; and second, most of the predictive power resides in <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, as models using only <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math> perform considerably worse. These findings empirically support our design: <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> captures task-relevant information, while <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math> enables learning from incomplete or unlabeled data and models structured variability not directly related to the predictive task. To verify that the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> spaces were truly independent, as implied by the structure of their variational posterior means, we calculated the cosine similarity between every dimension of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and every component of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. The highest similarity we observed was <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.032" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.032</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.032</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming that the two spaces are effectively orthogonal.</p>
</div>
